title: For Cleo
pid: e94d5cb1-3af2-4757-ab19-fe4de8663621
body: |2-
  What the work changed in her territory, to hand over whole.
  - Emacs: 'C-c v p' and 'skg-switch-overrides-followed' are gone; 'skg-override-perspective' on 'C-c v o', in 'elisp/skg-request-override-perspective.el'. The buffer keeps two permanent-locals, 'skg-overrides-followed' (the wire name, nil for the root authors') and 'skg-override-perspective-name' (the server's name, for the mode line); 'skg--set-override-perspective' sets both, and every reuse of a buffer for a fresh view must call it with nils (the search does).
  - Neovim: 'nvim/lua/skg/override_perspective.lua'; 'buffer.set_override_perspective'; the statusline function is now 'buffer.override_perspective_mode_line', so a statusline that called the old name breaks.
  - The clients always send "next" and print "Perspective: NAME." from the answer's 'perspective-name'; they never compute a perspective.
  - A replaced root keeps its viewnode ID, so point on it survives a switch both ways; content overrides switched back still get a new ID, as her "A redraw keeps point and folds" says.
  - skg-for-ai: 'override-perspective' replaces 'follow-overrides'; the sidecar carries 'perspective_name' beside 'overrides_followed', and every carry site copies both through 'perspective_fields_of_sidecar'.
  - 'C-c s d' became 'C-c s e' (Neovim 'se'); the server's messages name the new key, and 'nvim/lua/skg/messages.lua' translates it.
  - Her notes still name 'C-c v p', 'follow-overrides' and 'overrides_followed_mode_line' in "what I know".
